The KNN model reached 71.9% accuracy, but this number is misleading.
The test set has 96 rows (74 dry, 22 rainy), so a model that always predicts "no rain" would score about 77%, meaning KNN did slightly worse than guessing. 
The confusion matrix shows why: it caught only 4 of the 22 rainy hours (rain recall 0.18, precision 0.31, F1 0.23) and missed 18. 
It looks good on dry hours (recall 0.88) because it mostly predicts the majority class. The macro F1 of 0.53 is close to random for a two-class problem. In short, KNN with these settings has learned very little about rain,
and an person should read its accuracy as a result of class imbalance, not skill.
